1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,660
Walt is fifty eight, and he's leaning on

2
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:04,920
the counter of his hardware store,

3
00:00:05,080 --> 00:00:07,160
because his chest feels like something heavy

4
00:00:07,240 --> 00:00:08,280
is sitting on it.

5
00:00:08,981 --> 00:00:12,496
A customer has already called nine one one.

6
00:00:12,576 --> 00:00:13,856
In the next twenty minutes,

7
00:00:13,936 --> 00:00:16,461
four trained people are going to help him,

8
00:00:16,581 --> 00:00:18,301
and each of them is allowed to do something

9
00:00:18,381 --> 00:00:20,261
the one before was not.

10
00:00:20,967 --> 00:00:23,188
Priya is one of them.

11
00:00:23,268 --> 00:00:26,033
She's an emt, and she has aspirin in her

12
00:00:26,193 --> 00:00:28,113
bag that could help Walt right now.

13
00:00:28,913 --> 00:00:31,393
So is she allowed to just give it...

14
00:00:31,473 --> 00:00:33,753
or does she have to ask somebody first?

15
00:00:35,124 --> 00:00:36,404
By the end of this video,

16
00:00:36,484 --> 00:00:38,324
you'll know the four levels of providers

17
00:00:38,404 --> 00:00:41,419
in ems, which stands for emergency medical

18
00:00:41,579 --> 00:00:42,379
services.

19
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:45,325
You'll know what each level adds,

20
00:00:45,405 --> 00:00:47,725
what an emt is responsible for on every

21
00:00:47,885 --> 00:00:50,685
call, and who gives an emt permission to

22
00:00:50,765 --> 00:00:51,565
treat a patient.

23
00:00:52,765 --> 00:00:54,845
So why should you care about this before

24
00:00:54,925 --> 00:00:56,445
you've touched a single patient?

25
00:00:57,206 --> 00:00:59,926
Everything you'll ever do as an emt happens

26
00:01:00,006 --> 00:01:01,686
inside the ems system.

27
00:01:02,486 --> 00:01:04,326
That system decides what you're allowed

28
00:01:04,366 --> 00:01:06,366
to do and who you answer to.

29
00:01:07,092 --> 00:01:09,588
The national exam asks about it too,

30
00:01:09,668 --> 00:01:11,828
and those questions get much easier once

31
00:01:11,908 --> 00:01:13,508
you see how the pieces fit.

32
00:01:17,308 --> 00:01:19,148
We'll start with who shows up.

33
00:01:19,904 --> 00:01:21,904
People who give care before the hospital

34
00:01:21,944 --> 00:01:23,824
in the United States are trained to one

35
00:01:23,904 --> 00:01:26,629
of four national levels.

36
00:01:26,709 --> 00:01:28,629
Each level includes everything in the level

37
00:01:28,789 --> 00:01:30,709
below it and then adds more,

38
00:01:30,869 --> 00:01:32,789
so you can picture them as four steps on

39
00:01:32,829 --> 00:01:35,244
a staircase.

40
00:01:35,324 --> 00:01:37,324
The first step is the emergency medical

41
00:01:37,404 --> 00:01:40,860
responder, or emr.

42
00:01:40,940 --> 00:01:43,340
An emr is often the first trained person

43
00:01:43,420 --> 00:01:46,360
on scene, and many of them are police officers

44
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:48,560
or firefighters.

45
00:01:48,640 --> 00:01:51,440
An emr calls in more help and gives basic

46
00:01:51,520 --> 00:01:55,015
care for the problems that kill fastest.

47
00:01:55,095 --> 00:01:57,051
That means opening the airway,

48
00:01:57,131 --> 00:01:59,531
which is the path air takes to the lungs,

49
00:01:59,611 --> 00:02:02,801
stopping heavy bleeding, and starting cpr

50
00:02:02,881 --> 00:02:05,926
with an aed, a machine that can shock a

51
00:02:05,966 --> 00:02:07,486
heart out of a deadly rhythm.

52
00:02:08,667 --> 00:02:10,907
The second step is the emergency medical

53
00:02:10,987 --> 00:02:14,918
technician, the emt, and that's the level

54
00:02:14,998 --> 00:02:15,958
you're training for.

55
00:02:16,739 --> 00:02:19,219
In most places, it's the minimum certification

56
00:02:19,299 --> 00:02:20,579
for staffing an ambulance.

57
00:02:21,379 --> 00:02:24,259
An emt assesses the patient and gives basic

58
00:02:24,419 --> 00:02:26,059
care for illness and injury.

59
00:02:26,795 --> 00:02:29,115
That care includes oxygen and a short list

60
00:02:29,195 --> 00:02:31,720
of medications, and some of those are the

61
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:34,400
patient's own prescriptions.

62
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:37,040
Then the emt transports the patient to the

63
00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:38,160
hospital.

64
00:02:38,240 --> 00:02:41,320
So what does the third step add?

65
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:44,110
That step is the advanced emt,

66
00:02:44,190 --> 00:02:46,551
or A emt.

67
00:02:46,591 --> 00:02:48,951
An advanced emt does everything that an

68
00:02:49,031 --> 00:02:53,811
emt does, and then adds a few advanced skills.

69
00:02:53,891 --> 00:02:56,681
An A emt can start an iv,

70
00:02:56,761 --> 00:02:58,792
which is a line into a vein,

71
00:02:58,872 --> 00:03:01,752
or an io, which is a line into a bone,

72
00:03:01,832 --> 00:03:04,632
so fluids can go straight in.

73
00:03:04,712 --> 00:03:07,352
An A emt can also place certain advanced

74
00:03:07,432 --> 00:03:12,232
airway devices and give several more medications.

75
00:03:12,312 --> 00:03:14,552
The top step is the paramedic.

76
00:03:15,262 --> 00:03:17,182
That's the highest of the four levels.

77
00:03:17,898 --> 00:03:20,538
A paramedic has all the skills of the emt

78
00:03:20,858 --> 00:03:22,458
and of the advanced emt.

79
00:03:23,258 --> 00:03:26,354
On top of those, a paramedic adds advanced

80
00:03:26,674 --> 00:03:28,354
assessment and decision making,

81
00:03:28,474 --> 00:03:30,434
like reading a tracing of the heart's electrical

82
00:03:30,514 --> 00:03:33,074
signals and choosing from a much longer

83
00:03:33,234 --> 00:03:34,394
list of drugs.

84
00:03:35,894 --> 00:03:38,054
Those four steps are about to arrive at

85
00:03:38,134 --> 00:03:38,934
Walt's store.

86
00:03:39,710 --> 00:03:41,870
Deputy Ruiz, from the sheriff's office,

87
00:03:41,990 --> 00:03:42,830
gets there first.

88
00:03:43,566 --> 00:03:46,446
He's an emr, so he sits Walt down,

89
00:03:46,486 --> 00:03:49,916
keeps him calm, and sets an aed within reach.

90
00:03:50,702 --> 00:03:53,543
Priya's ambulance pulls up next.

91
00:03:53,623 --> 00:03:55,923
She tells Walt her name, and she starts

92
00:03:56,003 --> 00:03:58,563
her assessment while her partner Marcus,

93
00:03:58,603 --> 00:04:02,844
who's an advanced emt, gets an iv ready.

94
00:04:02,924 --> 00:04:05,324
A paramedic named Joan is already driving

95
00:04:05,404 --> 00:04:06,404
out to meet them.

96
00:04:07,204 --> 00:04:10,604
So Walt gets four people at four levels,

97
00:04:10,684 --> 00:04:12,844
and each one adds something the last one

98
00:04:12,924 --> 00:04:15,064
couldn't.

99
00:04:15,224 --> 00:04:17,104
Now it's your turn.

100
00:04:17,184 --> 00:04:19,424
A patient needs an iv started so she can

101
00:04:19,504 --> 00:04:21,050
get fluids.

102
00:04:21,130 --> 00:04:23,130
What's the lowest level of provider who

103
00:04:23,210 --> 00:04:24,890
can start it?

104
00:04:24,970 --> 00:04:27,370
And which level is the usual minimum for

105
00:04:27,450 --> 00:04:28,810
staffing an ambulance?

106
00:04:36,027 --> 00:04:39,378
The iv belongs to the advanced emt,

107
00:04:39,458 --> 00:04:41,618
because lines into a vein start at that

108
00:04:41,778 --> 00:04:42,578
third step.

109
00:04:43,269 --> 00:04:45,829
The minimum for the ambulance is the emt.

110
00:04:46,629 --> 00:04:49,376
The trap is assuming that anyone on an ambulance

111
00:04:49,496 --> 00:04:51,681
can start an iv.

112
00:04:51,761 --> 00:04:54,381
An emt gives important care,

113
00:04:54,461 --> 00:04:56,141
but under the national model,

114
00:04:56,221 --> 00:04:58,381
that skill sits one step higher.

115
00:05:02,181 --> 00:05:05,461
So what is an emt actually responsible for

116
00:05:05,501 --> 00:05:06,301
on a call?

117
00:05:06,957 --> 00:05:10,583
The list has an order, and the order matters.

118
00:05:11,308 --> 00:05:13,854
Your own safety comes first,

119
00:05:13,894 --> 00:05:15,734
and I know that can sound selfish.

120
00:05:16,468 --> 00:05:19,364
But an injured emt can't help anybody,

121
00:05:19,760 --> 00:05:22,975
and now the scene has two patients.

122
00:05:23,095 --> 00:05:26,376
Your crew comes next, for the same reason.

123
00:05:27,091 --> 00:05:28,851
The patient comes after that,

124
00:05:29,246 --> 00:05:32,207
and then the bystanders.

125
00:05:32,287 --> 00:05:35,107
Once the scene is safe, assessment always

126
00:05:35,187 --> 00:05:37,952
comes before care, because you can't treat

127
00:05:38,032 --> 00:05:40,072
what you haven't found.

128
00:05:40,272 --> 00:05:42,552
After care, you lift and move the patient

129
00:05:42,592 --> 00:05:45,627
safely, then you transport,

130
00:05:45,747 --> 00:05:48,907
and then you transfer care at the hospital.

131
00:05:49,067 --> 00:05:51,307
You never leave a patient before that handoff

132
00:05:51,387 --> 00:05:53,073
is complete.

133
00:05:53,153 --> 00:05:55,578
And through all of it, you speak up for

134
00:05:55,658 --> 00:05:58,018
what the patient needs, which is called

135
00:05:58,178 --> 00:05:59,978
being the patient's advocate.

136
00:06:01,084 --> 00:06:02,844
Running through that list isn't enough by

137
00:06:02,964 --> 00:06:05,929
itself, though, because the job also takes

138
00:06:06,009 --> 00:06:06,809
judgment.

139
00:06:07,415 --> 00:06:10,476
To think like an emt, you gather information

140
00:06:10,556 --> 00:06:13,011
from the scene, from your assessment,

141
00:06:13,131 --> 00:06:15,891
and from the patient, and then you put all

142
00:06:15,971 --> 00:06:17,691
of that together to decide.

143
00:06:18,327 --> 00:06:20,532
When you don't have enough information,

144
00:06:20,612 --> 00:06:23,252
you ask more questions or examine the patient

145
00:06:23,332 --> 00:06:24,852
further before you act.

146
00:06:26,263 --> 00:06:28,863
Priya is doing exactly that with Walt.

147
00:06:29,519 --> 00:06:31,359
Before she reaches for the aspirin,

148
00:06:31,439 --> 00:06:34,119
she asks about allergies and bleeding problems.

149
00:06:34,785 --> 00:06:37,235
Aspirin makes blood clot less,

150
00:06:37,315 --> 00:06:39,555
which can help the heart but is dangerous

151
00:06:39,635 --> 00:06:42,555
for someone who already bleeds easily.

152
00:06:42,755 --> 00:06:44,715
Then she has to think about where Walt should

153
00:06:44,875 --> 00:06:46,195
go.

154
00:06:46,275 --> 00:06:48,355
A heart attack happens when an artery that

155
00:06:48,435 --> 00:06:50,998
feeds the heart gets blocked.

156
00:06:51,024 --> 00:06:53,911
The nearest hospital is eight minutes away,

157
00:06:54,031 --> 00:06:55,911
but it doesn't have the lab that can open

158
00:06:55,991 --> 00:06:57,911
that artery with a thin tube.

159
00:06:58,621 --> 00:07:00,941
A heart center that does is twenty five

160
00:07:01,101 --> 00:07:02,061
minutes away.

161
00:07:02,823 --> 00:07:05,156
The closest hospital isn't always the right

162
00:07:05,236 --> 00:07:06,116
one...

163
00:07:06,276 --> 00:07:09,042
so Priya's crew heads for the heart center,

164
00:07:09,122 --> 00:07:12,662
just as their local rules direct.

165
00:07:12,862 --> 00:07:15,102
And what does the job ask from you,

166
00:07:15,182 --> 00:07:17,227
the person doing it?

167
00:07:17,307 --> 00:07:19,467
A commonly cited benchmark is being able

168
00:07:19,587 --> 00:07:21,707
to lift and carry about a hundred and twenty

169
00:07:21,867 --> 00:07:23,558
five pounds.

170
00:07:23,638 --> 00:07:25,638
You need good eyesight, including color

171
00:07:25,718 --> 00:07:28,803
vision, since lips or skin turning pale

172
00:07:28,883 --> 00:07:31,528
or blue is a real clue.

173
00:07:31,608 --> 00:07:34,968
A good emt also stays calm under pressure,

174
00:07:35,088 --> 00:07:37,608
listens without judging, and treats every

175
00:07:37,848 --> 00:07:39,208
patient fairly.

176
00:07:40,309 --> 00:07:42,669
This one's for you to try.

177
00:07:42,749 --> 00:07:46,259
You pull up to a car crash on a dark highway.

178
00:07:46,299 --> 00:07:48,379
The driver is slumped over the wheel,

179
00:07:48,459 --> 00:07:52,379
fuel is leaking, and traffic is still passing.

180
00:07:52,459 --> 00:07:55,219
What's your first responsibility?

181
00:07:55,339 --> 00:07:56,979
And once you reach a patient,

182
00:07:57,059 --> 00:07:59,485
which one should usually come first,

183
00:07:59,565 --> 00:08:01,245
assessment or care?

184
00:08:08,463 --> 00:08:11,583
Your first responsibility is your own safety,

185
00:08:11,663 --> 00:08:14,063
so you deal with the traffic and the fuel

186
00:08:14,303 --> 00:08:15,783
before you go to the driver.

187
00:08:16,583 --> 00:08:19,015
The trap is running straight to the patient,

188
00:08:19,095 --> 00:08:21,895
which is the instinct almost everyone has.

189
00:08:22,135 --> 00:08:25,245
Then, as a general rule, you assess before

190
00:08:25,325 --> 00:08:27,705
you treat, because you can't treat what

191
00:08:27,785 --> 00:08:28,905
you haven't found.

192
00:08:32,705 --> 00:08:34,385
That brings us back to the question from

193
00:08:34,465 --> 00:08:36,160
the start.

194
00:08:36,240 --> 00:08:38,400
Who says Priya can give Walt anything at

195
00:08:38,480 --> 00:08:40,141
all?

196
00:08:40,221 --> 00:08:42,781
Every ems system operates under the authority

197
00:08:42,861 --> 00:08:45,021
of a physician, who is called the Medical

198
00:08:45,101 --> 00:08:46,771
Director.

199
00:08:46,811 --> 00:08:49,371
The Medical Director has the final responsibility

200
00:08:49,451 --> 00:08:52,151
for patient care in that system.

201
00:08:52,191 --> 00:08:54,244
This physician writes the protocols,

202
00:08:54,298 --> 00:08:55,951
which are the written plans for treating

203
00:08:56,031 --> 00:08:58,351
each kind of problem, and also oversees

204
00:08:58,431 --> 00:09:01,712
the training and helps review the care.

205
00:09:01,752 --> 00:09:03,832
When an emt treats a patient,

206
00:09:03,912 --> 00:09:08,237
that emt is acting on the physician's authority.

207
00:09:08,277 --> 00:09:10,730
That authority reaches you in two ways,

208
00:09:10,784 --> 00:09:14,367
and together they're called medical direction.

209
00:09:14,407 --> 00:09:16,940
The first is off line medical direction,

210
00:09:16,994 --> 00:09:18,967
which means standing orders.

211
00:09:19,047 --> 00:09:20,647
Those are written instructions that let

212
00:09:20,727 --> 00:09:22,607
you act without calling anyone,

213
00:09:22,647 --> 00:09:25,762
so nobody has to be on the line with you.

214
00:09:25,802 --> 00:09:28,798
The second is on line medical direction,

215
00:09:28,838 --> 00:09:30,918
where a physician on duty gives you an order

216
00:09:31,038 --> 00:09:35,388
in real time, over the phone or the radio.

217
00:09:35,428 --> 00:09:38,543
Here's how both kinds look with Walt.

218
00:09:38,583 --> 00:09:40,823
Priya's protocols include a standing order

219
00:09:40,903 --> 00:09:42,663
for chest pain that might be coming from

220
00:09:42,743 --> 00:09:43,756
the heart.

221
00:09:43,810 --> 00:09:46,143
Once she's checked for allergies and bleeding,

222
00:09:46,183 --> 00:09:48,423
she hands him four small aspirin tablets

223
00:09:48,503 --> 00:09:50,128
to chew.

224
00:09:50,168 --> 00:09:51,955
She doesn't have to ask anyone...

225
00:09:52,061 --> 00:09:53,848
because the physician already gave that

226
00:09:54,008 --> 00:09:56,024
order in writing.

227
00:09:56,064 --> 00:09:58,464
Walt also carries his own nitroglycerin

228
00:09:58,624 --> 00:10:00,194
tablets.

229
00:10:00,234 --> 00:10:02,314
Nitroglycerin can drop a patient's blood

230
00:10:02,634 --> 00:10:04,514
pressure, so in Priya's system,

231
00:10:04,554 --> 00:10:07,639
the protocol says to call first.

232
00:10:07,679 --> 00:10:09,572
She gets a physician on the radio,

233
00:10:09,706 --> 00:10:11,214
reports what she's found,

234
00:10:11,254 --> 00:10:13,414
and hears the order before she helps Walt

235
00:10:13,494 --> 00:10:16,022
take one.

236
00:10:16,076 --> 00:10:17,969
The Medical Director also checks how the

237
00:10:18,049 --> 00:10:19,809
care went.

238
00:10:19,889 --> 00:10:22,649
After a call, the emt writes a report,

239
00:10:22,689 --> 00:10:24,529
and the Medical Director's team reviews

240
00:10:24,729 --> 00:10:26,929
reports like that one to find problems and

241
00:10:27,049 --> 00:10:28,610
fix them.

242
00:10:28,679 --> 00:10:31,079
So the physician you rarely see is there

243
00:10:31,279 --> 00:10:33,479
before the call, in the protocols,

244
00:10:33,559 --> 00:10:37,168
and after the call, in the review.

245
00:10:37,235 --> 00:10:39,345
It's your turn again.

246
00:10:39,385 --> 00:10:42,025
Naloxone is a drug that reverses an opioid

247
00:10:42,145 --> 00:10:43,705
overdose.

248
00:10:43,745 --> 00:10:45,585
Your protocol says that you may give it

249
00:10:45,665 --> 00:10:47,945
to a patient with a suspected overdose,

250
00:10:47,985 --> 00:10:50,665
and you don't need to call anyone.

251
00:10:50,705 --> 00:10:52,305
Which kind of medical direction are you

252
00:10:52,385 --> 00:10:53,165
using?

253
00:10:53,185 --> 00:10:55,265
And if you radio a physician for an order

254
00:10:55,345 --> 00:10:57,265
instead, which kind is that?

255
00:11:04,458 --> 00:11:07,258
The naloxone is off line medical direction,

256
00:11:07,378 --> 00:11:09,789
because it's a standing order.

257
00:11:09,869 --> 00:11:12,749
The radio call is on line medical direction,

258
00:11:12,869 --> 00:11:14,829
since the order comes from a physician in

259
00:11:14,909 --> 00:11:15,789
real time.

260
00:11:16,589 --> 00:11:18,589
This is where people slip.

261
00:11:18,669 --> 00:11:20,829
They think that off line means no doctor

262
00:11:20,869 --> 00:11:22,639
is involved.

263
00:11:22,679 --> 00:11:24,479
The physician is involved,

264
00:11:24,559 --> 00:11:26,692
and simply gave the order ahead of time,

265
00:11:26,746 --> 00:11:27,546
in writing.

266
00:11:31,319 --> 00:11:34,784
We can put the whole system in one picture.

267
00:11:34,864 --> 00:11:37,640
There are four levels, the emergency medical

268
00:11:37,720 --> 00:11:42,200
responder, the emt, the advanced emt,

269
00:11:42,240 --> 00:11:45,011
and the paramedic, and each one adds to

270
00:11:45,091 --> 00:11:47,051
the level below.

271
00:11:47,131 --> 00:11:49,986
On a call, your safety comes first,

272
00:11:50,026 --> 00:11:53,032
and assessment comes before care.

273
00:11:53,112 --> 00:11:55,192
And a physician called the Medical Director

274
00:11:55,272 --> 00:11:57,727
stands behind everything you do,

275
00:11:57,847 --> 00:12:00,602
through standing orders, which are off line,

276
00:12:00,682 --> 00:12:02,648
and through real time orders,

277
00:12:02,728 --> 00:12:03,768
which are on line.

278
00:12:07,568 --> 00:12:09,648
Joan meets the ambulance on the road and

279
00:12:09,728 --> 00:12:10,648
climbs in.

280
00:12:11,343 --> 00:12:13,503
Marcus already has the iv running,

281
00:12:13,583 --> 00:12:15,023
so the line is ready for her.

282
00:12:15,823 --> 00:12:18,294
Twenty minutes after the call for help,

283
00:12:18,374 --> 00:12:20,134
she reads Walt's heart tracing,

284
00:12:20,514 --> 00:12:22,274
and she warns the heart center that he's

285
00:12:22,474 --> 00:12:23,274
coming.

286
00:12:23,765 --> 00:12:26,245
Four people at four levels took care of

287
00:12:26,365 --> 00:12:28,880
him, and a Medical Director none of them

288
00:12:29,040 --> 00:12:31,680
saw that day stood behind every step.

289
00:12:32,474 --> 00:12:35,127
The heart center opens his blocked artery,

290
00:12:35,507 --> 00:12:38,027
and Walt goes home three days later.

291
00:12:38,743 --> 00:12:40,423
You'll find the notes and practice for this

292
00:12:40,503 --> 00:12:43,969
topic, free, on levlprep.com.

293
00:12:44,740 --> 00:12:47,030
And next time, we'll cover how you stay

294
00:12:47,110 --> 00:12:50,585
certified, how ems checks its own care,

295
00:12:50,976 --> 00:12:54,256
and why Priya never gave Walt any oxygen.

